# A moon does not only name a world — it names what KIND of world, and says so before you take it.
#
# Ten variants shift the Perlin thresholds the terrain is read through: where the waterline sits, how
# high the treeline is, how much of the land is worth farming. Which one a moon gets is a pure
# function of its world seed, which is what lets the selection box print it beside the seed rather
# than only after the world has been built.
#
# Deliberately runs WITHOUT --seed, for the reason world_selection.cli does: that flag names the
# world on the command line and skips the screen this is about. Still reproducible — the sky is drawn
# from a constant, so moon 0 is Armoth in every process, and Armoth's seed hashes to the same variant
# every time.
#
# What is asserted, and what is not: that the box names the variant before CONFIRM, that the run
# really is built to it, and that the variant is not forced. NOT how much sea or forest that variant
# produces — that is --world-variant-audit's job, over sixteen worlds per variant, and pinning a
# biome share here would turn every calibration pass into a red test that says nothing about whether
# the wiring works.
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --debug --no-encounters --location-type test --location-id 0 --npc-static --period noon

wait
click menu New
wait mode WorldSelection 20

# Nothing chosen: the World row is empty like the rest of them. The box draws every row in every
# state, so the label is on screen with nothing beside it.
expect World

# Armoth is moon 0, its seed is -209322086, and that seed hashes to Insular. Named in the box
# BEFORE the world exists — the whole point of deriving the variant from the seed rather than from
# the generator.
click moon Armoth
wait
expect Insular
expect small land, strewn across the water

# Take it. The world is generated here.
click world confirm
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60

# The run really is that world, and the variant was not forced by a flag — `seed-names` is what the
# seed would have chosen on its own, and it agrees.
expect-state world-variant id=insular
expect-state world-variant forced=no
expect-state world-variant seed-names=insular
expect-no-state world-variant (no world generated)

# The shape reached the generator rather than sitting in a table unread: Insular is the baseline
# with the continents broken small and the waterline raised, so its continent scale is well under
# the baseline's 12.
expect-state world-variant continent=7.0
expect-state world-variant settlement=3.0

# And it is on the world line beside the region, which is where a script asking one question about
# where the avatar stands will look.
world
world-variant

quit
